# The web page viewer: `browse` shows HTML files from the disk and pages
# from the web in a window. It is driven here from the host (keys typed
# into its window, the text of the windows read back).
@ai
@net
@http
$ ai control on
$ mkdir /site
$ echo "<title>Bee pages</title><h1>Welcome</h1><p>Go to <a href=two.html>page two</a> or <a href='https://10.0.2.2:1/'>a secure one</a>.<ul><li>first<li>second</ul>" > /site/index.html
$ echo "<p>This is <b>page two</b>. <a href=../site/index.html>Home</a>" > /site/two.html
$ browse /site/index.html
/site/index.html in a new window: Tab picks a link, Enter follows it, q leaves
$ threads
~ browse     (waiting|ready)
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^--- window 1 ---$
~ ^ Bee pages - /site/index\.html  \(100%\)$
~ ^Welcome$
~ ^Go to page two or a secure one\.$
~ ^  \* first$
~ ^  \* second$
~ Tab link  Enter/click follow  Backspace back

# Tab picks a link, Enter follows it; Backspace comes back.
@ctl keys {machine} "{tab}{enter}"
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^ /site/two\.html  \(100%\)$
~ ^This is page two\. Home$
@ctl keys {machine} "{backspace}"
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^ Bee pages - /site/index\.html
# A secure page is opened like any other (TLS 1.3); this server is not
# there, and the viewer says so and stays.
@ctl keys {machine} "{tab}{tab}{enter}"
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^ https://10\.0\.2\.2:1/: 10\.0\.2\.2 port 1: connection refused
~ ^Welcome$

# `g` asks for an address: here a file on the disk.
@ctl keys {machine} "g/site/two.html{enter}"
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^ /site/two\.html  \(100%\)$
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ threads
!~ browse

# A page from the web, in a window of its own; a missing page says why.
$ net dhcp
~ ^address 10\.0\.2\.15/24 \(DHCP\)
$ browse http://{http}/hello
~ ^http://10\.0\.2\.2:\d+/hello in a new window
$ sleep 1
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^ http://10\.0\.2\.2:\d+/hello  \(100%\)$
~ ^Hello from the test web server$
$ browse {http}/nothing-here
~ ^http://10\.0\.2\.2:\d+/nothing-here in a new window
$ sleep 1
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^ http://10\.0\.2\.2:\d+/nothing-here: \S
$ browse
usage: browse URL|FILE|WORDS   (words: a search on FrogFind)
